{"id":"bf3b3301-b852-4708-883b-620630788afd","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022648,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_set:\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_set and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. It's about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" on a parse tree (nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). The objective: reduce the tree (remove nodes) while preserving required symbols (required_symbols list). The algorithm: deeper nodes first, then shallower; never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained node IDs and actual removal order (removed_node_ids in order of removal?). The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" (array of strings) and \"retained_node_ids\" (array of strings). The function should return that JSON object.\n\nFrom the description: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" – we need to simulate a reduction algorithm. We have a tree: nodes list each with id, parent (null for root), removable (bool), symbol, depth. Also utility (float) but not clear if used except maybe for ordering? The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" Then \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to process nodes in order: descending depth, then ascending node ID? Or ascending depth? Deeper first means higher depth value first. Then tie break by node ID (string? likely alphabetical order). But ID like \"n1\", \"n2\" so lexicographic works.\n\nAlgorithm: For each node in that order, if it is removable and its removal does not violate required symbols (i.e., the set of symbols present in the retained nodes after removal still includes all required_symbols), and it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its descendants are removed or not? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained, cannot remove parent. But we process deeper first, so children may be removed before parent. So we need to track which nodes are retained. Initially all nodes that are not removable? Actually root is not removable, other nodes may be removable. The algorithm: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove nodes that have children (descendants) that are still retained. Since we process deepest first, children are considered before parent. So when considering a node, if any of its children (direct children by parent id) are still retained, we cannot remove it. But also we must check \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after hypothetical removal, the set of symbols of retained nodes still covers required_symbols.\n\nSo we need to simulate: start with all nodes as retained? Actually the goal is to remove nodes that are removable, while preserving required symbols. So we consider them for removal. The process: iterate nodes in order (descending depth, tie id). For each node, if removable, and (its symbol is not required? Actually never remove required symbol is stated separately: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node with symbol that is in required_symbols cannot be removed. Also if it has any children that are still retained, cannot remove. Also if removing it would cause some required symbol to disappear from the set of symbols of all retained nodes (including ancestors, siblings, etc.) then cannot remove. So we need to maintain a set of retained node ids and their symbols. Initially all nodes are retained. Then for each node in order, check conditions. If conditions met, we remove it: remove from retained set. We record the removal order (the order in which they are removed). After processing all nodes, we output removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted alphabetical? \"Return sorted retained IDs\" – so sort alphabetically or by ID order? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (likely ascending string order). Removed_node_ids in the order they were removed.\n\nCheck edge cases: root (id \"root\", depth 0, removable false). Required symbols: \"root\" is always required? Not necessarily, but root symbol is \"root\" and likely required. The required_symbols list includes \"root\" and \"s1\". So root is required symbol. So never removed.\n\nAlso note: \"removable\" field indicates if the node can be considered for removal. Non-removable nodes are always retained.\n\nImportant: The algorithm says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removing this node, the set of symbols of the remaining retained nodes must still include all required_symbols. So we need to compute the current symbols of retained nodes, and check if after removal, the required set is still covered.\n\nAlso \"node with retained children\" – we need to check if any direct child (by parent id) is still in retained set. Since we process deeper first, children might have been removed already, so this condition may allow parent removal if all children already removed.\n\nWe also need to consider that a node might be removed even if its symbol is required? No, required symbol nodes are explicitly never removed. So skip those.\n\nNow, the description also mentions \"utility\" but not used in the algorithm explicitly. Possibly for future? But not needed for this task.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that takes case payload (an object with nodes list and required_symbols list). Must return dict with removed_node_ids (list) and retained_node_ids (list). Must not mutate case.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse nodes: create dict mapping id to node info. Also create adjacency: for each node, store list of children ids (by parent). Also create map from id to node.\n\n2. Initialize retained set: all node ids that are not removable? Actually initially all nodes are retained? But the algorithm starts with all nodes? It says \"Tentatively remove a node only if...\" implies we start with all nodes retained, then try to remove. But are non-removable nodes ever removed? No. So we can start with retained set = set of all node ids. But we only consider removal for removable nodes. So we will iterate over all nodes in order (depth desc, id asc). For each node, if removable and not required symbol and no retained children and removal doesn't break required symbols, then remove.\n\nBut careful: We need to check \"node with retained children\" meaning if any direct child is still in retained set. So we need to know children for each node. Also we need to consider that a node might have children that are not yet considered? Since we process deeper first, children (deeper) are processed before parent, so if a child is removable and gets removed, then when we reach parent, that child is no longer retained. So we need to dynamically update retained set.\n\nAlso need to track removal order: append node id when removed.\n\nAfter loop, compute retained_node_ids sorted (ascending string order). removed_node_ids already in removal order.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not removable? They are always retained. So they will be in retained set.\n\nWhat about nodes that are removable but never removed? They stay retained.\n\nWe need to ensure we process all nodes, including root? Root is not removable, so no effect.\n\nNow, check required symbols condition: The required_symbols list may contain symbols that are represented by multiple nodes. We need to ensure that after removal, the set of symbols of the union of retained nodes still contains all required symbols. So we need to maintain a set or multiset? It's a set: we just need each required symbol present at least once among retained nodes. So we can maintain a Counter or set: for each required symbol, the count of retained nodes with that symbol. Initially count for each symbol. When we consider removing a node, we decrement count for its symbol, and if any required symbol count goes to zero, then cannot remove (restore count). Else we can remove (keep decremented). So we can do that.\n\nAlso note: required_symbols is list of strings. The root has symbol \"root\". There may be multiple nodes with same symbol.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Build nodes_dict: {id: node}\n- Build children_dict: {parent_id: list of child ids} for all nodes.\n- Build symbol_counts: dict from symbol to int, counting all nodes initially.\n- Initialize removed_ids = [].\n- Retained set: initially all node ids (can be derived from nodes list). We'll track retained set as we remove.\n\n- Ordering: we need list of node ids sorted by (depth descending, id ascending). Since depth is integer, we can sort with key=lambda nid: (-nodes_dict[nid]['depth'], nid). But careful: sorting strings: \"n1\" vs \"n2\" vs \"n10\" - lexicographic will put \"n10\" before \"n2\" because '1' < '2'. Is that intended? The problem says \"node ID as the stable tie break\". Usually node IDs are like \"n1\",\"n2\",...,\"n10\" - lexical sort would not be numeric. Should we treat as numeric? In the fixture, IDs are \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"n6\". Not \"n10\". So lexical is fine. But to be safe, we can assume IDs are strings and sort lexicographically. The problem says \"use node ID as the stable tie break\". No mention of numeric conversion. So use string comparison.\n\n- For each nid in sorted_nodes:\n  - node = nodes_dict[nid]\n  - if not node['removable']: continue\n  - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set: continue (required symbol)\n  - if node['parent'] is not None: check children? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child (by parent field) is still retained. We have children_dict from parent mapping. So we can check for each child_id in children_dict.get(nid, []): if child_id in retained_set, then cannot remove.\n  - Also for root? root has parent null, no children? It has children. But root is not removable anyway.\n  - If has retained children: continue\n  - Then check required_symbol condition: symbol_counts[node['symbol']] - 1 >= 1 for all required symbols? Actually we need to see if after decrement, all required symbols have count >= 1. So we can check: new_count = symbol_counts[node['symbol']] - 1; if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set and new_count == 0: then removing would make that symbol missing. But also other required symbols unaffected. So condition: for req_sym in required_symbols_set: if req_sym == node['symbol'] then need new_count >= 1; else need symbol_counts[req_sym] >= 1 (already true). So essentially, if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set and symbol_counts[node['symbol']] == 1: cannot remove. Because after removal, count becomes 0.\n  - If condition passes, then remove:\n    - retained_set.remove(nid)\n    - removed_ids.append(nid)\n    - symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\n\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained_set)) (lexicographic).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nEdge cases: what if a node with required symbol is removable and has duplicate symbol? The condition \"never remove a required symbol\" is absolute: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So even if there are multiple nodes with same required symbol, we should not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols set. Because it says \"a required symbol\" meaning the node's symbol is required. So we should not remove any node with symbol in required_symbols. That is simpler: just skip all nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols_set. This overrides the count condition. So we don't need to consider count for required symbols; just never remove them. So we can skip those nodes entirely.\n\nBut careful: The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So yes, nodes whose symbol is one of the required_symbols are never removed. So we can just filter them out from consideration.\n\nThus we don't need to track symbol_counts for required symbols? Actually we still need to ensure that after removals, the required symbols are present. But since we never remove any node with required symbol, the required symbols will always be present because at least one node with that symbol remains (the one that was never removed). However, what if all nodes with a required symbol are removable? But we never remove them, so they remain. So condition is automatically satisfied. However, we must ensure that we don't remove a node that is the only node providing a required symbol (if that node is not required-symbol itself but another node with same symbol? Wait: required symbols list says which symbols must be present. If a symbol is required, we must have at least one node with that symbol. But we never remove nodes with that symbol. So it's fine. But what if a required symbol is only present on non-removable nodes? That's fine. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied because we never remove nodes with required symbols. However, there might be a scenario where a required symbol is present only on a node that is removable but not required? But that node's symbol is not in required_symbols, so it could be removed, causing that symbol to disappear. But if that symbol is required, it must be in required_symbols. So the nodes with that symbol would be required and not removed. So no issue.\n\nBut wait: The required_symbols list might contain symbols that are also present on nodes that are not themselves marked with that symbol? No, symbol is per node. So if a symbol is required, any node with that symbol is considered a required symbol node? The phrasing \"a required symbol\" could mean a node whose symbol is in the required set. So we never remove such nodes. Thus the required symbols condition is automatically satisfied. However, the algorithm also says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This is a separate condition. But given we never remove required-symbol nodes, it's redundant. But we can still check for safety. But let's follow the exact description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we skip those. Then for tentatively removable nodes, we check if after removal all required symbols still represented. But since we never remove required symbol nodes, the only way a required symbol could disappear is if we remove a node that is the last node with that required symbol? But that node's symbol is required, so we wouldn't consider it. So it's safe. So we can just skip required symbol nodes and not check the condition. But to be thorough, we can still check and if it would fail, skip. It won't happen.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case). The function will receive the payload object (dict with keys: nodes, required_symbols, _case_nonce). We'll extract those.\n\nAlso, note: the output schema says removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids are arrays of strings. So we must return those.\n\nNow, regarding the strict submission envelope: we need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being a string of Python source code. The Python source must be valid and define solve(case). Must not import anything beyond standard library. Must not mutate case. We'll write the function.\n\nWe must ensure we handle possible missing keys? The payload always has those keys as per fixtures.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: sorting of retained node IDs: we need to sort using default string sort. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure we create copies of data structures to avoid mutating input. For nodes list, we can iterate over nodes and create dicts.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\n\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n\n    # ordering: descending depth, then id ascending (string)\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\n\n    removed_order = []\n\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_set:\n            # never remove required symbol\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children)\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols representation after removal\n        # since we never remove required symbol nodes, this is automatically true but let's check\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        # If this symbol is required, we already skipped, so sym not in required_set\n        # But if there are other required symbols, they are unaffected.\n        # So condition always holds. However, if we accidentally had a node with required symbol that was not skipped (maybe because removable? But we skip), condition would fail.\n        # So we can skip the check or do it for safety.\n        # We'll do the check: after removing, does any required symbol have count 0?\n        # Since we never remove required-symbol nodes, required symbols always have at least the nodes that were never removed.\n        # But let's compute:\n        # new_counts = symbol_counts.copy(); new_counts[sym] -= 1; but that's mutable.\n        # Instead just check if sym in required_set and symbol_counts[sym] == 1: then would become 0.\n        if sym in required_set and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\n            continue  # cannot remove the only node with a required symbol (but sym is required, shouldn't happen)\n        # Actually if sym not in required_set, no problem.\n        # For safety, we can also check all required symbols:\n        # can_remove = True\n        # for req in required_set:\n        #     if req == sym:\n        #         if symbol_counts[req] - 1 <= 0:\n        #             can_remove = False\n        #     else:\n        #         if symbol_counts[req] <= 0: # should not happen initially\n        #             can_remove = False\n        # if not can_remove: continue\n        # But given our skip, it's unnecessary.\n        # We'll implement the safe check to be robust.\n\n        # remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)  # default string sort\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nBut note: We need to import nothing. Also, be careful about children_map: if node has no children, we don't add entry. Use .get().\n\nWe need to handle case where parent might be None for root. children_map for root will have entries if others have parent='root'.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure that the root's removable is False, so it stays.\n\nNow, test with fixture? We cannot run but we can reason. For fixture case 0:\n\nNodes: root(0), n1(1,s1,removal false), n2(1,s2,removable true), n3(1,s0,false), n4(2,s1,removable true,parent n2), n5(2,s2,false,parent n3), n6(2,s0,removable true,parent n1). Required: root, s1.\n\nSorted by depth descending: depth=2: n4(n2), n5(n3), n6(n1). Depth=1: n1, n2, n3. Depth=0: root.\n\nProcessing order: depth2 first. Tiebreak by id: n4, n5, n6.\n\nn4: removable true, symbol s1 is in required_set? Yes, s1 is required. So skip (never remove required symbol). n5: removable false, skip. n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, parent n1. Children of n6? none? Actually n6 has no children (only depth2 with no deeper). So no retained children. Required symbols check: s0 not required, so fine. Remove n6. retained.remove('n6'), removed_order=['n6'], symbol_counts['s0']-- (from 2 to 1? There is s0 at n3 and n6, so after removal, s0 count=1). Continue.\n\nDepth1: n1: removable false, skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4 is a child of n2, and n4 is still retained (since we skipped it). So has retained child -> cannot remove n2. n3: removable false, skip.\n\nDepth0: root: removable false, skip.\n\nRemoved: ['n6']. Retained: all except n6, sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? But root id is \"root\", so after sorting: 'n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root'? Lexicographically: 'n1' < 'n2' < 'n3' < 'n4' < 'n5' < 'root' because 'r' > 'n'? Actually 'r' > 'n', so 'root' comes after all 'n' ids. So order: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is this the expected output? The fixture doesn't provide expected output. But we trust algorithm.\n\nNow, check case 1 and 2 similar.\n\nPotential issue: what if a removable node has children that are removable and later removed, then when we reach parent, those children are no longer retained, so parent might become removable. Our order processes parent after children, so that's fine. For n2, child n4 is retained (skipped because required symbol), so n2 cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nNow, consider if there was a node with required symbol that is removable? We skip, so it's never removed. Good.\n\nOne more nuance: The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That we did. And \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Done.\n\nNow, output format: JSON with removed_node_ids (list) and retained_node_ids (list). Must be in that order? Schema says properties: removed_node_ids, retained_node_ids. No order requirement. We'll return dict with those keys.\n\nNow, we need to produce the JSON string for \"implementation\". So we need to write Python source code as a string. We'll need to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes. The easiest is to write the code in a multi-line string and then use json.dumps to encode it. But we are writing the final output directly as JSON. Since we are generating JSON, we need to produce a valid JSON object with a string value that contains the Python source. We'll have to manually escape or rely on our own serialization. I'll write the Python source as a multiline string in the response, then ensure the JSON is properly escaped.\n\nGiven the output must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\", we can construct the JSON manually. But careful with newlines: JSON strings can contain newlines escaped as \\n. So we need to replace actual newlines with \\n. Also backslashes and double quotes need escaping.\n\nI'll write the Python code as a string with triple quotes inside, but the outer JSON string will need double quotes. So we'll need to escape internal double quotes as \\\". I'll write the code using single quotes for internal strings to avoid escaping issues, but we must be careful: in Python, we can use double quotes or single quotes. For the outer JSON, we need to wrap the entire Python source in double quotes. So any double quote inside the Python source must be escaped. I'll use single quotes for strings inside Python to reduce escaping. Also ensure that backslashes for \\n are literal newlines in the Python source but in the JSON string they need to be \\n. So we can write the Python code with actual newlines, then JSON-encode it.\n\nSince we are producing the final JSON directly in the response, we can write it as a string with proper escaping. I'll use a Python script to generate the JSON, but here we need to manually produce.\n\nLet me construct the Python code as a string:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).a