\nThus we can construct the JSON as:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_map.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_set:\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_set and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"}\n\nBut we need to ensure the JSON string is valid. Check for any unescaped characters: The code uses single quotes, which are fine. No double quotes. The backslash only appears in escaped sequences we inserted (\\n). So it's valid.\n\nHowever, note that inside the code, there is a line: `children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)` That's fine.\n\nAlso, we used `symbol_counts.get(sym, 0)` — that's fine.\n\nNow, the final output must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementa